The `path` parameter in `writeFileTo` (and `writeFileToAsync` on line 122) is used directly without sanitization. In a ZIP extraction context, a malicious archive entry with a path like `../../etc/passwd` or `../sensitive-file` can escape the intended target directory — a classic "Zip Slip" vulnerability. Before writing, resolve the path against the intended base directory and verify it stays within bounds:

```js
var resolvedPath = pth.resolve(baseDir, path);
if (!resolvedPath.startsWith(pth.resolve(baseDir) + pth.sep)) {
    throw new Error('Path traversal detected: ' + path);
}
```

Even after fixing `cleanUrl`, there is no check that the resolved `relativePath` stays within the base `path` directory. An attacker who bypasses the URL sanitization (e.g., via symlinks, or if `cleanUrl` has gaps) could read or write arbitrary files on the server. Add a `path.resolve`-based confinement check before any filesystem operation:

```js
var resolvedBase = require('path').resolve(path);
var resolvedTarget = require('path').resolve(relativePath);
if (resolvedTarget.indexOf(resolvedBase) !== 0) {
    writeError('forbidden', 403);
    return;
}
```

The `.then` chain on `pdfImage.numberOfPages()` has no `.catch`, so a rejection from `numberOfPages()` leaves the outer promise permanently pending. Replace the pattern with:

```js
pdfImage.numberOfPages().then(function (totalPages) {
  // ...existing body...
}).catch(function (error) {
  reject(error);
});
```

None of the `async` route handlers in this file have `try/catch` blocks. Any unhandled promise rejection (e.g., a MongoDB connection error) will propagate as an unhandled rejection and can crash the Node.js process. Wrap each async handler body in a `try/catch` and pass errors to `next(err)` or return an appropriate error response. For example, the `/firstuser` handler should be:

```js
router.post('/firstuser', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    // ... handler body ...
  } catch (err) {
    res.status(500).json({ success: false, message: 'Internal server error.' })
  }
})
```

This applies to `/firstuser`, `/setpassword`, `/forgotpassword`, `/firstinstall`, and `/verify`.

If `options` includes a `stdio` configuration that doesn't pipe `stdout` or `stderr` (e.g., `stdio: 'inherit'` or `stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', 'inherit']`), then `child.stdout` and/or `child.stderr` will be `null`, and calling `.on('data', ...)` on them will throw a `TypeError` at runtime. Add null guards before attaching these listeners:

```js
if (child.stdout) {
  child.stdout.on('data', function(data) { stdout += data; });
}
if (child.stderr) {
  child.stderr.on('data', function(data) { errorMessage += data; });
}
```

`DuplexStream.call(this)` is invoked **after** `this.on('pipe', ...)` and `this.on('unpipe', ...)` are registered (lines 22–27). This is a genuine bug: `DuplexStream.call(this)` eventually calls `EventEmitter.init`, which resets `this._events` to a fresh object, wiping out any listeners registered before the super-constructor runs. As a result, the `pipe`/`unpipe` handlers that set up error forwarding will never fire, and the `_callback` will never be invoked on stream errors.

Move `DuplexStream.call(this)` to the very top of the constructor body, before any `this.on(...)` calls or `this.append(...)` calls:

```js
function BufferList (callback) {
  if (!(this instanceof BufferList))
    return new BufferList(callback)

  DuplexStream.call(this)  // initialize stream state first

  this._bufs  = []
  this.length = 0
  // ... rest of constructor
}
```
